This gives me the exact established pattern for refactoring a diff-scanner: a state-machine with small pure helpers, each with complexity ≤ 8, heavily unit-tested.

Note an important semantic difference: `postTestReviewComments.isLinePresentInDiff` returns **true** for empty inputs (`if (!diffText || !filePath || !targetLine) return true;` — permissive default meaning "can't validate, allow"), whereas `postPReviewComments.parseDiffLineInfo` returns `{present: true}` for empty inputs too. Actually the same permissive default! Good.

But careful: semantics differ slightly in how they treat `+++ /dev/null`, `-` lines, context lines, etc. `postTestReviewComments.isLinePresentInDiff`:
- `diff --git ` resets currentFile=null, newLine=null (but NOT oldLine — there is no oldLine concept)
- `+++ b/` sets currentFile (any `+++` line — including `+++ /dev/null`? `line.indexOf('+++ b/') === 0` — `+++ /dev/null` does NOT start with `+++ b/`, so it's not treated as a file marker; it stays unhandled... then falls to the else branch `newLine++`? Wait — but only if currentFile === filePath. If currentFile !== filePath → `continue` happens before. Let me re-read:

```js
if (line.indexOf('diff --git ') === 0) { currentFile = null; newLine = null; continue; }
if (line.indexOf('+++ b/') === 0) { currentFile = line.substring('+++ b/'.length); continue; }
if (currentFile !== filePath) continue;
var hunk = line.match(/^@@ -\d+(?:,\d+)? \+(\d+)(?:,\d+)? @@/);
if (hunk) { newLine = parseInt(hunk[1], 10); continue; }
if (newLine === null) continue;
if (line.indexOf('+') === 0 || line.indexOf(' ') === 0) {
   if (newLine === lineNumber) return true;
   newLine++;
} else if (line.indexOf('-') === 0) {
   continue;
} else if (line === '\\ No newline at end of file') {
   continue;
} else {
   newLine++;
}
``

So:
- `--- a/...` lines: not matched by any header check → if currentFile === filePath and newLine !== null → falls to else → newLine++ (bug-ish but that's behavior — `---` starts with `-`, so it goes to the `-` branch! `line.indexOf('-') === 0` — yes `--- a/foo` starts with `-`. OK good.)
- Deleted files (`+++ /dev/null`): currentFile stays whatever previous file was... e.g. `--- a/foo` doesn't set currentFile. So for deleted files the function can't match. Behavior: only `+++ b/` sets file. Keep exact.

The safest refactor: keep the same scanning semantics, decompose into small helpers that each handle one aspect, in the same file (like gh-928 did in postTestReworkResults) or mirror postPReviewComments' decomposition. Since postPReviewComments.isLinePresentInDiff already exists (with a `side` param) — could postTestReviewComments just delegate? No! Semantics differ:

postPReviewComments.parseDiffLineInfo handles deleted files (`+++ /dev/null` → currentFile = oldFile → can match LEFT lines), whereas postTestReviewComments's version returns true on empty args (can't validate) and false otherwise; postPReviewComments's version also returns present:true on empty args. Difference: for `+++ /dev/null` case: postTestReviewComments would NOT find lines of deleted files (no `+++ b/`), postPReviewComments would. And for context lines, postTestReviewComments returns true if newLine === lineNumber (RIGHT-side match); postPReviewComments checks both sides. Also `--- a/foo` line: postTestReviewComments treats it via `-` branch (oldLine counting is absent, just skips), postPReviewComments treats it as a header. Since scan results can differ on some diffs, delegation risks a behavior change. The ticket says "Behaviour unchanged". So: decompose in place, miroring the structure of postPReviewComments but keeping semantics identical to the current postTestReviewComments implementation.

Also worth noting: `postInlineComment` in postTestReviewComments uses `isLinePresentInDiff(diffText, filePath, inlineComment.line)` and falls back to a PR comment when the line is NOT present. Keep that.

