### isLinePresentInDiff redesign (final):

```js
function newInlineScanState() {
   return { currentFile: null, newLine: null };
}

/** Header bookeeping: 'diff --git' resets the scan, '+++ b/' switches file. */
function trackInlineHeaderLine(st, line) {
   if (line.indexOf('diff --git ') === 0) {
       st.currentFile = null;
       st.newLine = null;
       return;
   }
   if (line.indexOf('+++ b/') === 0) {
       st.currentFile = line.substring('+++ b/'.length);
   }

/** The new-side start line of a hunk header, or null when the line is not one. */
function inlineHunkStartOf(line) {
   var hunk = line.match(/^@@ -\d+(?:,\d+)? \+(\d+)(?:,\d+)? @@/);
   return hunk ? parseInt(hunk[1], 10) : null;
}

/** Content line inside the target file segment: does it land on lineNumber? */
function inlineContentLineMatches(st, line, lineNumber) {
   if (line.indexOf('+') === 0 || line.indexOf(' ') === 0) {
       if (st.newLine === lineNumber) return true;
       st.newLine++;
       return false;
   }
   if (line.indexOf('-') === 0) return false;
   if (line === '\\ No newline at end of file') return false;
   st.newLine++;
   return false;
}

function scanInlineLinePresence(lines, filePath, lineNumber) {
   var st = newInlineScanState();
   for (var i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
       var line = lines[i];
       trackInlineHeaderLine(st, line);
       if (st.currentFile !== filePath) continue;
       var hunkStart = inlineHunkStartOf(line);
       if (hunkStart !== null) {
           st.newLine = hunkStart;
           continue;
       }
       if (st.newLine === null) continue;
       if (inlineContentLineMatches(st, line, lineNumber)) return true;
   }
   return false;
}

function isLinePresentInDiff(diffText, filePath, targetLine) {
   if (!diffText || !filePath || !targetLine) return true;
   var lineNumber = parseInt(targetLine, 10);
   if (!lineNumber) return true;
   return scanInlineLinePresence(String(diffText).split(/\r?\n/), filePath, lineNumber);
}
``

CRITICAL check — original: `if (line.indexOf('+++ b/') === 0) { currentFile = ...; continue; }` then `if (currentFile !== filePath) continue;`. Mine: after trackInlineHeaderLine, `st.currentFile !== filePath` — if +++ b/ just set currentFile===filePath, we fall through to hunkStart check for THAT line ('+++ b/...' → null), then `st.newLine === null` → continue (newLine only set by hunks; but WAIT — could newLine be non-null when a new file starts? 'diff --git' resets newLine=null before each '+++ b/'. And the +++ line itself arrives only after a diff --git reset... unless diff text starts mid-file without 'diff --git' (weird). If newLine was non-null and currentFile switches via +++ b/ to filePath, then for the +++ b/ line: hunkStart null → newLine not null → inlineContentLineMatches('+++ b/...', ...): starts with '+' → if newLine===lineNumber return true; newLine++. In the ORIGINAL: the '+++ b/' line hits `continue` at the header check → newLine unchanged, then next line... Both same?? Original: '+++ b/x' matches the header check FIRST (before content), so continue — newLine stays whatever it was. Mine: header sets currentFile, falls through to content handling: '+' prefix → newLine++ or match. DIFFERENT if newLine was non-null at that point. When can newLine be non-null while hitting a '+++ b/' line? Between 'diff --git' (resets null) and '+++ b/', newLine can only be set by a hunk line — hunks come after +++ lines. So in any well-formed diff, newLine is null at '+++ b/' → both continue → identical. But for MALFORMED input ('+++ b/x' without preceding 'diff --git' after a previous file's hunk): original keeps old newLine & skips the +++ line entirely; mine would consume the +++ line as a '+' content line. Edge-case divergence on malformed diffs. The ticket demands behavior unchanged — safest to keep the exact `continue` semantics: have trackInlineHeaderLine RETURN a boolean "consumed":

